// ---------- T8 Justice: truthfulness and lying (ST II-II qq.109–113; with qq.40 a.3, 60, 69, 72–76), four lessons ----------
// Summa quotations: Dominican translation (1920), checked by script against New Advent. Scripture: Douay-Rheims. Evidence checked on PubMed (29 Oct 2026).
addTerms([
 {id:"truthfulness",t:"Truthfulness (the virtue of truth)",la:"veritas, veracitas",d:"Moral theology",b:"The moral virtue by which a person shows himself outwardly, in words and deeds, as he is inwardly, saying what he holds to be true.",p:"Truth in things is a conformity of mind and reality; truthfulness is the virtue of making one's signs conform to one's mind (II-II q.109 a.1). It is a potential part of justice: directed to another and establishing an equality (of sign to thought), but owed as a moral rather than a legal debt, since \"it would be impossible for men to live together, unless they believed one another\" (q.109 a.3 ad 1).",ex:"Saying 'I don't know' when you don't, rather than bluffing.",where:"II-II q.109",ccc:"2468–2470",conf:"Telling everything (the virtue includes discretion); or factual accuracy alone (one can say something false without lying).",rel:["justice","lie"],lesson:"t8tr1",alias:["truthfulness","veracity","honesty","virtue of truth"]},
 {id:"lie",t:"Lie",la:"mendacium",d:"Moral theology",b:"An assertion of what one believes to be false, made with the will to state the false (and ordinarily the intention to deceive).",p:"Thomas: falsehood can be material (what is said is false), formal (the will to say what is false) and effective (the intention to deceive); \"the essential notion of a lie is taken from formal falsehood\" (II-II q.110 a.1). Augustine: \"a false statement uttered with the intention of deceiving\". CCC 2482: \"A lie consists in speaking a falsehood with the intention of deceiving.\" Every lie is a sin, since words are natural signs of thought (q.110 a.3).",ex:"Telling a lecturer you were ill when you simply forgot the deadline.",where:"II-II q.110",ccc:"2482–2487",conf:"An honest mistake (material falsehood only); a joke both sides know is a joke; fiction; withholding.",rel:["truthfulness","mental-reservation"],lesson:"t8tr1",alias:["lie","lying","mendacium","falsehood","untruth","white lie"]},
 {id:"mental-reservation",t:"Mental reservation",la:"restrictio mentalis",d:"Moral theology",b:"Limiting the meaning of one's words in one's mind. 'Broad' reservation uses words whose limited sense a hearer could reasonably grasp from context; 'strict' reservation adds a purely private qualification no one could detect.",p:"Strict mental reservation is, in effect, a lie; propositions defending it were condemned by Innocent XI in 1679. Broad mental reservation (e.g. 'Mrs X is not at home' meaning 'not receiving visitors', understood by social convention) has been widely held permissible for proportionate reason. Many contemporary Thomists treat even broad reservation cautiously.",ex:"A doctor asked by a stranger about a patient: 'I can't say anything about that.'",where:"Innocent XI (1679); Newman, Apologia, Note G",ccc:"2488–2489",conf:"Silence and refusal to answer, which are not reservations.",rel:["lie","secret"],lesson:"t8tr3",alias:["mental reservation","equivocation","restrictio mentalis","strict mental reservation","broad mental reservation"]},
 {id:"detraction",t:"Detraction, calumny and rash judgment",la:"detractio, calumnia, iudicium temerarium",d:"Moral theology",b:"Detraction: disclosing another's real faults to those who did not know, without objectively valid reason. Calumny: harming another's reputation by falsehood. Rash judgment: assuming, without sufficient basis, the moral fault of another.",p:"CCC 2477. Thomas: backbiting is speaking ill of an absent person to blacken his good name, by its nature a mortal sin, since a good name is \"the most precious\" of temporal goods (II-II q.73 a.2); \"unless we have evident indications of a person's wickedness, we ought to deem him good, by interpreting for the best whatever is doubtful about him\" (q.60 a.4). Those who damage reputation must make reparation (CCC 2487).",ex:"Telling classmates about a friend's past failing that they had no need to know.",where:"II-II qq.60, 72–76",ccc:"2477–2479, 2487",conf:"Necessary disclosure (e.g. to protect someone, report abuse, give a reference honestly).",rel:["lie","justice"],lesson:"t8tr4",alias:["detraction","backbiting","calumny","slander","rash judgment","gossip","tale-bearing"]},
 {id:"secret",t:"Secret and the right to the truth",la:"secretum",d:"Moral theology",b:"Information one is bound to keep undisclosed: by nature, by promise, or by profession; and the principle that not everyone has a right to every truth.",p:"\"The right to the communication of the truth is not unconditional\"; \"No one is bound to reveal the truth to someone who does not have the right to know it\" (CCC 2488–2489). The seal of confession is inviolable (CCC 2490); professional secrets must be kept save in exceptional cases of grave harm (CCC 2491). Keeping a secret is done by silence or discretion, never by lying.",ex:"A counsellor declining to tell a parent what an adult client said.",where:"II-II q.69 a.2; q.110 a.3 ad 4",ccc:"2488–2492",conf:"A right to deceive those without a right to the truth (a disputed view).",rel:["confidentiality","mental-reservation"],lesson:"t8tr3",alias:["secret","professional secret","right to the truth","discretion","keeping secrets"]}
]);

